<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Empire of Thessalonica</h1><p class="st-meta">1224 CE – 1246 CE · capital Thessalonica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Greek empire founded when Theodore Komnenos Doukas of Epirus took Thessalonica in 1224 and claimed the Byzantine throne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>, ruler of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-epirus">Despotate of Epirus</a>, steadily conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-thessalonica">Kingdom of Thessalonica</a> and captured the city in 1224. Around 1227 he was crowned emperor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrios-chomatenos">Demetrios Chomatenos</a>, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, challenging the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> for leadership of the Byzantine world and preparing to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Theodore ruled a realm stretching from the Adriatic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, with Byzantine court titles and administration, and quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea's</a> patriarch over church authority. In 1230 he attacked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> but was defeated and captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klokotnitsa">Klokotnitsa</a> and later blinded. His brother Manuel then ruled as a Bulgarian client.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Theodore regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> for his son John, who in 1242 had to accept the lesser title of despot from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Vatatzes</a>. In 1246 Vatatzes took Thessalonica from John's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrios-angelos-doukas">Demetrios</a>, annexing it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/empire-of-thessalonica.jpg" alt="Empire of Thessalonica" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epirus_1205-1230-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1224 CE – 1246 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thessalonica</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Theodore Komnenos Doukas, Manuel Komnenos Doukas, John Komnenos Doukas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Despotate of Thessalonica</dd></dl></aside></div>
